      "evidence": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:1880aab419cda5e033fa",
          "block_id": "block:2ce7919828b3d420e9d3",
          "page": null,
          "section": "LLM Generating Commands to Steal Documents and System Information",
          "excerpt": "In June, GTIG identified the Russian government-backed actor APT28 (aka FROZENLAKE) using new malware against Ukraine we track as PROMPTSTEAL and reported by CERT-UA as LAMEHUG . PROMPTSTEAL is a data miner, which queries an LLM (Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct) to generate commands for execution via the API for Hugging Face, a platform for open-source machine learning including LLMs. APT28's use of PROMPTSTEAL constitutes our first observation of malware querying an LLM deployed in live operations.",
          "excerpt_sha256": "5d755f1d10cd5d16cb5aab1b53f27fd2a98c0af0bd93f6151e68a006c53d2e4c"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:116649faa9ac4ba8b62b",
          "block_id": "block:2475df205ffda94586c0",
          "page": null,
          "section": "Threat Actors Developing Novel AI Capabilities",
          "excerpt": "Observed in operations",
          "excerpt_sha256": "5e384db50b438cb391cbcf3422b5143f7f7ee18900f73498c1ada3fe4b71348b"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:be1a15e42259cfd64bfd",
          "block_id": "block:af7e58a6cc4764697fc4",
          "page": null,
          "section": "Threat Actors Developing Novel AI Capabilities",
          "excerpt": "Data miner written in Python and packaged with PyInstaller. It contains a compiled script that uses the Hugging Face API to query the LLM Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct to generate one-line Windows commands. Prompts used to generate the commands indicate that it aims to collect system information and documents in specific folders. PROMPTSTEAL then executes the commands and sends the collected data to an adversary-controlled server.",
          "excerpt_sha256": "06fde3bc39b8d7012f670a3c3a3c1fb32573a6ebffc82f37f6b2a85ca21fff0e"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:dad7d73a19765a942a81",
          "block_id": "block:0b2e04b516f89c3705c9",
          "page": null,
          "section": "Key Findings",
          "excerpt": "\"Social Engineering\" to Bypass Safeguards: Threat actors are adopting social engineering-like pretexts in their prompts to bypass AI safety guardrails. We observed actors posing as students in a \"capture-the-flag\" competition or as cybersecurity researchers to persuade Gemini to provide information that would otherwise be blocked, enabling tool development.",
          "excerpt_sha256": "bda3c2142472255fbe6a47e25300b2aef365086c346c6bdb78104445a7e92d40"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:df4943fb5c1af6ee44d8",
          "block_id": "block:0a2ac37983eebefa147b",
          "page": null,
          "section": "LLM Generating Commands to Steal Documents and System Information",
          "excerpt": "PROMPTSTEAL novelly uses LLMs to generate commands for the malware to execute rather than hard coding the commands directly in the malware itself. It masquerades as an \"image generation\" program that guides the user through a series of prompts to generate images while querying the Hugging Face API to generate commands for execution in the background.",
          "excerpt_sha256": "fefdaf5e9e8641fc5e52b9b798b6a41ddff83d33c23eb0d0c203058caf8bfd29"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:4003386a6ebecc2bd7e8",
          "block_id": "block:6e4a66d66ecbf01d6b60",
          "page": null,
          "section": "LLM Generating Commands to Steal Documents and System Information",
          "excerpt": "Figure 2: PROMPTSTEAL prompt used to generate command to collect system information",
          "excerpt_sha256": "d54d1c53dbb0a6b9830170a018710349e01b2fcd386e6bcafdbe15de3b025242"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:a8c746b4bdf5ca1c39fc",
          "block_id": "block:5465e9d23382ce18a484",
          "page": null,
          "section": "LLM Generating Commands to Steal Documents and System Information",
          "excerpt": "Figure 3: PROMPTSTEAL prompt used to generate command to collect targeted documents",
          "excerpt_sha256": "2728ffa819895c1518fa3724b5efcf9e22b563f6353e5cec963e50d2aa4b3658"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:a51b688743d6468b3b86",
          "block_id": "block:cf56728f9176db0a458f",
          "page": null,
          "section": "LLM Generating Commands to Steal Documents and System Information",
          "excerpt": "PROMPTSTEAL likely uses stolen API tokens to query the Hugging Face API. The prompt specifically asks the LLM to output commands to generate system information and also to copy documents to a specified directory. The output from these commands are then blindly executed locally by PROMPTSTEAL before the output is exfiltrated. Our analysis indicates continued development of this malware, with new samples adding obfuscation and changing the C2 method.",
          "excerpt_sha256": "d997e7e670fedd9eccabb239875d09a7e82ab1351acd4c8588124520cdb46241"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "evidence:d7cd8e0ff0fb310bbe41",
          "block_id": "block:0ecb4525907c97d568ff",
          "page": null,
          "section": "Social Engineering to Bypass Safeguards",
          "excerpt": "Guided by our AI Principles , Google designs AI systems with robust security measures and strong safety guardrails. Threat actors are adopting social engineering-like pretexts in their prompts to bypass AI safety guardrails. We observed actors posing as participants in a \"capture-the-flag\" competition for cybersecurity researchers to persuade Gemini to provide information that would otherwise be blocked, enabling vulnerability exploitation and tool development. In addition to disrupting these actors, we use these insights to continuously improve our classifiers and strengthen the security of our products.",
          "excerpt_sha256": "84fea5be2b90bc0ed16b5e1dfca77e223a7492e6eeadae4b48758815ee9c4481"
        }
      ]
    },
